Subject: [PATCH] ASoC: qcom: x1e80100: lift the speaker volume caps

Upstream commit "ASoC: qcom: x1e80100: limit speaker volumes" (2026-04-22, in
v7.1 but not v7.0) clamps Digital Volume to -3 dB and PA Volume to 0 dB "to
reduce the risk of speaker damage until we have active speaker protection in
place".

On the Lenovo Yoga Slim 7x that leaves the speakers ~18 dB quieter than the
same hardware running v7.0 (measured on an Ubuntu 26.04 live USB with
7.0.0-14-generic: PA Volume 16/31 = +15 dB, Digital Volume 84/124 = 0 dB),
which is quiet enough to be unusable at full volume.

Raise both limits to the hardware maximum. The kernel then stops clamping and
the actual levels stay under userspace control, so volume can be lowered again
instantly with amixer/UCM if it distorts -- no rebuild required.

DELIBERATE LOCAL DEVIATION: this discards the protection upstream added. There
is still no excursion/thermal speaker protection on Linux for the WSA884x, so
sustained high-level playback can physically damage the speakers. The chosen
levels are the ones v7.0 shipped with and that this machine ran without
incident, but that is empirical, not a guarantee.

--- a/sound/soc/qcom/x1e80100.c
+++ b/sound/soc/qcom/x1e80100.c
@@ -35,20 +35,26 @@
 	case WSA_CODEC_DMA_RX_0:
 	case WSA_CODEC_DMA_RX_1:
 		/*
-		 * Set limit of -3 dB on Digital Volume and 0 dB on PA Volume
-		 * to reduce the risk of speaker damage until we have active
-		 * speaker protection in place.
+		 * Local: upstream limits Digital Volume to -3 dB (81) and PA
+		 * Volume to 0 dB (6) to reduce the risk of speaker damage until
+		 * active speaker protection exists.  That is ~18 dB quieter than
+		 * v7.0 on this machine, so lift both to the hardware maximum and
+		 * leave the actual level to userspace.
+		 *
+		 * WARNING: there is still no excursion/thermal protection for the
+		 * WSA884x on Linux.  Sustained loud playback can damage the
+		 * speakers.  Lower "PA Volume" with amixer if it distorts.
 		 */
-		snd_soc_limit_volume(card, "WSA WSA_RX0 Digital Volume", 81);
-		snd_soc_limit_volume(card, "WSA WSA_RX1 Digital Volume", 81);
-		snd_soc_limit_volume(card, "WSA2 WSA_RX0 Digital Volume", 81);
-		snd_soc_limit_volume(card, "WSA2 WSA_RX1 Digital Volume", 81);
-		snd_soc_limit_volume(card, "SpkrLeft PA Volume", 6);
-		snd_soc_limit_volume(card, "SpkrRight PA Volume", 6);
-		snd_soc_limit_volume(card, "WooferLeft PA Volume", 6);
-		snd_soc_limit_volume(card, "TweeterLeft PA Volume", 6);
-		snd_soc_limit_volume(card, "WooferRight PA Volume", 6);
-		snd_soc_limit_volume(card, "TweeterRight PA Volume", 6);
+		snd_soc_limit_volume(card, "WSA WSA_RX0 Digital Volume", 124);
+		snd_soc_limit_volume(card, "WSA WSA_RX1 Digital Volume", 124);
+		snd_soc_limit_volume(card, "WSA2 WSA_RX0 Digital Volume", 124);
+		snd_soc_limit_volume(card, "WSA2 WSA_RX1 Digital Volume", 124);
+		snd_soc_limit_volume(card, "SpkrLeft PA Volume", 31);
+		snd_soc_limit_volume(card, "SpkrRight PA Volume", 31);
+		snd_soc_limit_volume(card, "WooferLeft PA Volume", 31);
+		snd_soc_limit_volume(card, "TweeterLeft PA Volume", 31);
+		snd_soc_limit_volume(card, "WooferRight PA Volume", 31);
+		snd_soc_limit_volume(card, "TweeterRight PA Volume", 31);
 		break;
 	case DISPLAY_PORT_RX_0:
 		dp_pcm_id = 0;
